{"id": "ue914d5b9b9", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41578-026-00984-6", "source": "Nature Reviews Materials", "title": "Chemical defects in semiconducting polymers and their impact on reproducibility and device performance - Nature Reviews Materials", "byline": "Vanderspikken; Jochen; Beljonne; David; Sommer; Michael; Maes; Wouter; Müller; Christian", "date": "Nov 26", "image": "https://media.springernature.com/m685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig1_HTML.png", "thumb": "e914d5b9b9.jpg", "html": "<h2>Abstract</h2>\n    <p>Conjugated polymers are central to the field of organic electronics. The continuous drive to develop high-performance materials and explore alternative synthetic routes has led to unprecedented chemical versatility, allowing researchers to finely tune semiconducting polymer properties for specific applications. However, non-optimal polymerization conditions and/or limited differentiation between reacting monomers may facilitate side reactions, leading to the covalent incorporation of structural defects. Depending on their nature, these defects can perturb the electronic structure of conjugated polymers, causing varying degrees of deviation from the envisioned structure. As a result, structure–property relationships depend not only on molar mass, molar mass distribution and end groups but also on defect level. In this Perspective, we discuss these structural discrepancies and encourage researchers to critically evaluate the polymers they synthesize and study, recognizing structural variability as a potential driver of batch-to-batch inconsistency. We illustrate the origins of structural defects, outline strategies to limit or avoid them, and assess commonly used characterization techniques. Furthermore, we showcase the influence of structural irregularities on physicochemical and optoelectronic material properties, as well as device performance. Finally, we explore how structural defects can present opportunities, potentially offering new perspectives for materials design.</p>\n    <h2>Access options</h2>\n    <p>Access Nature and 54 other Nature Portfolio journals</p>\n    <p>Get Nature+, our best-value online-access subscription</p>\n    <p>Subscribe to this journal</p>\n    <p>Receive 12 digital issues and online access to articles</p>\n    <p>Buy this article</p>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Purchase on SpringerLink</li>\n      <li>Instant access to the full article PDF.</li>\n    </ul>\n    <p>USD 39.95</p>\n    <p>Prices may be subject to local taxes which are calculated during checkout</p>\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig1_HTML.png\" alt=\"Chemical schemes compare a correctly alternating polymer with possible structural variations. The polymer can be formed by DArP, CDC polymerization, or Stille, Suzuki, and Kumada coupling. Examples of variations include homocoupling or sequence defects, β-coupling, branching or cross-linking, different end groups, side reactions, isomerism, and regioregular or regio-irregular arrangements.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig2_HTML.png\" alt=\"Four panels illustrate methods for identifying monomer ratios and coupling defects in polymers. NMR compares chemical-shift peaks in homocoupling-induced samples, standard Stille samples and low-defect Suzuki samples. MALDI-ToF mass spectrometry distinguishes peaks associated with correct monomer ratios, monomer imbalance, homocoupling and end groups. ESD–STM images and molecular structures identify two defect types at 6% and 8%, alongside a molar-mass distribution. Model reactions show 90% cross-coupled product and two homocoupled products at 6% and 4%.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig3_HTML.png\" alt=\"A modelling workflow links defects in polymer chains to their electronic structure. Homocoupling and -coupling defects are used to determine single-chain formation energy and defect concentration, then defect distribution and spatially correlated positional disorder in chain assemblies. The assembly model is mapped onto a Hamiltonian. A cell schematic contrasts a main cell that is insufficient to represent the whole material with one that is sufficient. A density-of-states graph compares defect-free, uncorrelated-defect and correlated-defect cases. All three rise towards a peak near energy 2. The defect-free case has the highest peak; correlated defects have a lower peak and a second peak at higher energy, while uncorrelated defects extend farther into the high-energy tail.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig4_HTML.png\" alt=\"Chemical structures are grouped as cis Z and trans E isomers. The trans E structures are labelled 95.7–95.3% and 1.7–0.7%; the cis Z structures are labelled 2.6–3.6% and 0.0–0.4%. Two additional structures, AA prime and BB prime, are labelled 1–3% and 2–12%, respectively. Microscopy images below include molecular overlays; one marks alternating A and B rows. Both scale bars represent 2 nm.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig5_HTML.png\" alt=\"In the first panel, a general strategy to make conjugated polymers through DArP is shown. Factors listed for optimization include monomer purity, blocking and halogen reactivity; catalyst and ligand properties; base or acid; temperature; solvent; and polymer work-up, solubility, end-capping and metal scavenging. In the second panel, the monomers are redesigned to symmetric monomers, which are then coupled into a polymer by oxidative, Stille or Suzuki reactions, with different funcional groups specified for each method.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig6_HTML.png\" alt=\"Four panels compare polymer defects, annealing and device properties. Schematics contrast a broad molar-mass distribution with bromine-terminated chains and a narrower distribution with phenyl-terminated chains. Another schematic and microscopy images compare as-cast polymer:acceptor blend and annealed at 150 °C and 200 °C. Normalized external quantum efficiency curves compare defective and defect-free polymers at the three treatments; the defect-free exhibits higher absorption at longer wavelengths owing to a more intercalated morphology. A final graph shows diffusion length and short-circuit current density generally decreasing as the percentage of Cbz homocoupling increases.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/m312/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41578-026-00984-6/MediaObjects/41578_2026_984_Fig7_HTML.png\" alt=\"A reporting framework lists minimum data for monomer analysis, molar mass analysis and polymer characterization. Recommended additional data cover work-up, defect analysis and batch comparison. A radar chart compares Stille, Suzuki, DArP, CDC, and symmetric methods across reproducibility, homocoupling defects, β-defects, molar mass, synthetic complexity, scalability and general applicability. The methods have different profiles across these criteria; no numerical scale is shown.\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <h3>Similar content being viewed by others</h3>\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41467-025-68265-4/MediaObjects/41467_2025_68265_Fig1_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41557-025-01851-7/MediaObjects/41557_2025_1851_Figa_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <img src=\"https://media.springernature.com/w215h120/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41524-025-01683-6/MediaObjects/41524_2025_1683_Fig1_HTML.png\" loading=\"lazy\">\n    <h2>References</h2>\n    <p class=\"trimmed\">…</p>", "word_count": 264, "status": "full", "reason": null, "summary": "Synthesizing semiconducting polymers often introduces structural defects, altering optoelectronic properties and directly impairing device performance.", "extracted_at": "2026-11-27T05:38:06-0500", "http": "200"}